Date: Fri, 7 May 93 01:14:58 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech

When will a new chip plant cost more than anyone can raise? (Answer later on)

Yesterday I attended Intel's annual shareholder's meeting, in San Jose. I
got to talk to the only billionaire I know personally, Gordon Moore.

Most amazing was a set of charts showing the amount of money Intel will
spend to build the next generation of wafer fab plants.

Total cost of capital needed to build peak volume of 386:       $100 million
Total cost of capital needed to build peak volume of 486:        $1 billion
Total cost of capital needed to build peak volume of Pentium:  $5 billion

That is, Intel will have about $5 billion tied up in wafer fabrication
facilities for the Pentium (0.6 micron process) as peak volume production
is reached, around 1996.

(The Intel factory in Albuquerque is *already* the world's largest chip
plant, and is being *tripled* in size over the next two years.
Surprisingly, the other major chip companies, NEC, Fujitsu, TI, Motorola,
Toshiba, etc., have cut back their capital spending.)

If Intel has gauged market demands correctly, and does in fact keep this
capacity efficiently loaded, it will be by far the world's largest
electronics company in a few years--it is of course already the world's
largest chip company. If, on the other hand, it falters, then the crater it
makes will be equally impressive. 

Why is this of interest to Extropians?

1. To give you some flavor of the incredible investments required. The
economies of scale with a vengeance.

2. There are those who think nanotech is on the verge of displacing these
chips. (However, most folks on this list are quite a bit more reasonable
and know that the imminent death of silicon has been greatly exaggerated.)

3. It is always tempting to ask why some tiny fraction of a new chip plant,
say 1%, is not devoted to nanotech research. $10-20 million would pay for a
lot of research. The answer is complicated, but basically the investors and
planners clearly do not see a plausible return on such an investment. (I
did some calculations a while back, which Nick Szabo has seen and has
mentioned here, showing that "long shots"--which all of us will surely
agree basic research on nanotech is--must have incredibly high payoffs to
make up for the low probability of success. Standard calculations, of
course, but many enthusiasts for new technologies lose sight of this when
asking companies to be visionaries.)

The answer to the opening question is of course, "never." When chip plants
begin to cost $100 billion or so, probably around 2005 or later, this will
essentially be beyond the ability of existing capital markets to raise
(we'll in new financial territory at least). (Obviously, alternatives could
develop...maybe the plants won't be this expensive because smaller modules
will be feasible...etc.).

So, financing these high tech factories requires enormous amounts of money.
Extropians looking to make money should think about this.


-Tim 
