Date: Fri, 7 May 93 02:04:15 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Why does complicated accounting fail on this List?

This is a question I've been wondering about: Why do schemes involving
complicated accounting and pricing work in the "real world" but fail to
catch on on this list?

Several types of systems have been proposed, by Robin Hanson (various), by
Brian Hawthorne (HEx), and recently by Dani Eder. These schemes involve
such things as pricing posts, reputations, etc., in various combinations.
They get proposed, discussed, improved upon--but never implemented. 

Some possible reasons and comments:

1. Too complicated. Unlike, say, buying groceries or paying electric bills,
these schemes are complicated (I won't get into any in particular) and have
confusing rules, costs, benefits, etc. These markets are more similar to
complicated futures markets, which most folks avoid (I've bought puts and
calls, which are relatively simple, and yet I still have some qualms). The
challenge will be for us to design *very simple* systems with very simple
*things* being bought and sold. (Anything that involves a series of
calculations to figure out is not going to go over big here, in my
opinion).

2. The mail problem. People are using a variety of mail programs and so
have different communication constraints...witness the difficulty in
getting encryption made more universal or in getting beyond simple ASCII
for these messages. Any scheme that has payments attached to posts (even if
in only in "token" money)  will have to work robustly for a dozen or so
types of mail systems. (True, it can be done inside the ASCII text files,
manually in most cases, but this places the burden of accounting and
calculation on the user. Few people would use Visa cards if they had to
perform the necessary calculations, phone connections, etc.)

3. I doubt it's the *time* alone, as many of us spend far more time on this
list than we do dealing with conventional merchants.

4. However, some central accounting seems to be needed for some of the
schemes, as in the Eder plan, in which people get some number of credits
for each subscriber to their material. (Ideally, this would be
decentralized. The main way to decentralize such record-keeping is with
physical money. Thus, people would literally "buy" or "subscribe to" the
postings of others. Crypto money is an obvious route to explore, though
this is not ready yet for use.)

5. Perhaps the paradigms for dealing with ordinary goods are better
established, e.g., a book costs $5, a nice scalar quantity. Reputation
markets are likely to be much more complex and semantically confusing. (I
still don't know what the HEx system was supposed to measure, even though I
read Hawthorne's post a couple of times.)

6. Some people--like me, as my earlier posts showed--see too many ways to
exploit the rules of some abstract system to "break" the system. Simple
market purchases of commodities or other goods are less susceptable to this
rule trickery.

Any ideas on why these schemes never get off the ground? 

-Tim

