Subject: The Incredibly High Cost of High Tech

Tim May:
> That is, Intel will have about $5 billion tied up in wafer fabrication
> facilities for the Pentium (0.6 micron process) as peak volume production
> is reached, around 1996.

This is an interesting figure.  A figure interesting wrt market entry 
for new technologies is the cost of building the first plant.  What 
is the minimum Intel could spend if, today, they decided that instead
of increasing the market was going to be flat?  Copyright issues aside, 
what is the minimum cost for Cyrix or AMD to get into the Pentium 
business?  How does this compare to the entry costs for the 486 business?

I find it plausible that Intel may deliberately have set Pentium entry
costs as high to make it on hard for folks like Cyrix and AMD to
manufacture Pentium clones.   On the other hand, that would increase
Intel's exposure if Unix or Windows NT or Pink for RISC becomes the
desktop standard in five years.

Also interesting is the cost of chip fabs for SRAM,
DRAM, and ASICs.   What scale are these fabbed on these days?
Another interesting near-term nanotech-ish possibility
is using STMs to read/write hard disk drives at an atomic scale, recently 
announced in Japan (but currently the data rate is far too low).
It might be possible to diversify nanotech R&D over several industries.

> 3. It is always tempting to ask why some tiny fraction of a new chip plant,
> say 1%, is not devoted to nanotech research.  [high cost of long
> odds as the timeline goes longer]

The following is speculative and not very rigorous, I'm afraid,
but thrown out in the hopes that some of the ideas can be tossed 
around & improved upon:

A fab might spend $20 million on near-term nanotech research with an
overall cumulative chance of success (as defined below) of 10%, in
areas such as STM arrays, STM alignment, quantum dots, nanoresists, guided
ion-beam epitaxy, or other advanced submicron technologies.  "Success"
is specifically defined with f (performance, capital cost reduction) 
milestones achieved within, eg 10 years. The fab buys futures that pay off 
9:1 for "the house", which is a market of milestone-futures
speculators.

To simplify greatly, a milestone might be the reduction of
Pentium follow-on capital costs by $1 billion.  This gives the
fab an investment where their worst-case loss is ($20 million - $20 million - 
epsilon), while the expected win is 10%*($1,000 million - $200 million)
or $80 million.  The speculators carry the risk: they win $200 million
with a 10% chance, and lose ($20 million - interest + epsilon) with a 
90% chance.  Epsilon is the cost of interest plus the speculator's 
risk premium.

In an earlier discussion Robin Hanson pointed out the importance of
estimating the odds of success with such little information.  As
confidence in variance of the odds goes down, epsilon goes up --
something like a beta of beta.

We can further envision speculators diversifying their investments
across a wide range of technologies, companies, and industries.
Epsilon then becomes a function of the risk of ignored, unhedged
outsiders taking over the dominant technologies across a wide range
of industries within ten years, or that the entire future high-tech
economy goes south in a big way. This is not zero risk, but it
can be historically estimated.  Consistent over- or underestimation of 
the odds also increases epsilon, and this may be harder to deal with.

A problem faced by the speculator is that the fab might cut short the 
research for reasons other than not being able to meet the milestone;
eg the appearance of superior competition.   The odds of this increase 
because of moral hazard; the cost of the lab pulling out is only 
opportunity costs plus epsilon, since the speculators cover sunk R&D 
costs.  So the odds of failure must include the odds of a superior 
competitor technology and some moral hazard risk factor.  However, 
the existence of superior competition bodes well for the diversified 
speculator, as long as the total hedged R&D for the industry is not 
greater than what future demand will support.  Because of moral
hazard, odds should be set by independent "meta-research" houses, not by 
the researchers themselves.  This may entail significant loss of 
information given the proprietary nature of much of this research.
On the other hand the existence of R&D hedging made possible by 
such rating agencies gives companies incentive to make public their 
information, and thus may provide a superior, non-coercive replacement 
for the intended function of patents.

Alternatively, the house might let futures on the fab's patent
royalty stream.  (Here "royalties" might  also be some standardized 
method of mapping revenues to R&D costs, rather than actual patent
royalties). While less closely linked to specific R&D milestones,
it does account for the "spinoff effect"; eg STM arrays might fail
to reduce the cost or increase the performance of CPUs, but they
might be useful for SRAMs, or hard disk drives.  In fact it might
not be a fab doing the research at all, but university research
labs like LLNL, JPL, etc. which lets futures on its patent portfolio.

