OK, here's my attempt to value the World:

(1) Source: Statistical Abstract of the United States, US Dept. of
Commerce, 1992, table 735: Gross and Net Stock of Fixed Reproducible
Tangible Wealth in Current and Constant (1987) Dollar: 1970 to 1990

This table gives the net stock (depreciated value) of reproducible
wealth in the US as 14.891 trillion dollars in 1990.  To this we
must add land to find the total physical wealth.  At a crude estimate
of $10000 per capita, I estimate 2.5 trillion dollars for the land
value of the US.  So I have a total of 17.5 trillion dollars.

(2) Human labor is a priced asset.  We must therefore add the
present value of all the people's labor.  The GNP in 1990
was 5.47 trillion dollars.  Assuming that the average remaining
life of employees (as employees) is 20 years, and the discount
rate is 8%, then the present value of the working population is
9.8 times wage income.  I estimate wage income at 60% of GNP
(this value is obtainable, I just don't have it handy).  Then
the PV of wage income is 32 trillion.  Thus we see that most
of the value in the US is the PV of future wages, i.e. the people.

(3) The total value of the US obtained above is 47.1 trillion.
Since the US represents about 27% of the world GNP, then the
total value of the world can be estimated by ratio to the US
figure.  Europe may have more tangible assets per capita, and
Japan may have more land value, but other parts of the world
probably have less, so I will assume an equal ratio pending
a valuation of all the worlds assets.  Thus I get 176 trillion
dollars as the world value in 1990.

(4) Allowing 10% for inflation and real asset growth from 1990
to 1993, I get 193 trillion in 1993.  Given the innacuracies in
the estimates above (even national GNP statistics have some 
errors, and I have made some estimating errors above that), I
feel comfortable stating the value of the world at $200 trillion,
with an implied precision of +- 50 billion (i.e. one significant
figure).

(5) Now, to collect on the prize :-), Meridian Investment Company
(of which I am sole proprietor) now offers a new investment fund
the "World Fund".  By analogy to the SI system of units, which
uses 10^-7 of the distance from the equator to the pole as the
source of the meter, and the word 'meter' comes from the latin
root meaning measure; I define a unit of currency called the
'val', which is 10^-12 of the value of the Earth in 1993.  The
share price of the fund is 1.00 val, which will be attempted
to be maintained constant at that value.  The fund will invest
in physical assets and labor contracts in the proportion they
represent in the world.  Asset value growth will be paid out as
share dividends, so as to maintain the value of the shares at
1.00 val.  The fund has no minimum investment amount, but does
charge a 5 milli-val ($1 US) transaction charge for share purchases
and sales (to cover paperwork and postage).  There are no other
fees. The fund manager is paid 0.4% of fund balance or 5% of
investment income, whichever is less, for manageing the fund.
This is paid out of investment income and is not a load charge.

(6) In keeping with the goals of extropians, the fund is not
registered or licensed with any government body, as is true for
the Company as a whole.  I really have been managing investment
funds for others since August of 1987, and currently have $2000
under management (not counting my personal net worth of $142,000).


