rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu says:
>    Any biotech sytstem that can repair ruptured cells would certainly
> be advanced as nanotechnology.

Agreed.

> The problem with your scenario is that
> there is more than just freezing damage. Correct me if I am wrong Perry,
> but aren't the cryoprotectants that Alcor uses pumped in at over
> 10,000 times the toxicity level needed to kill?

No, from what I know the toxicity is actually fairly mild. On the
other hand, the cryoprotectants aren't that great. They are better
than nothing, certainly -- but they don't provide perfect
preservation. Significant ice crystal formation (an ice crystal
forming at the scale of a cell is like a giant sword that rips open
anything it forms through) and cracking (due to thermal stresses) of
tissues still occurs. Preliminary evidence is that this damage does
not eliminate the structural and other information that would be
needed to restore the damage -- but its fairly clear that you are
going to get very few individual cells or structures intact if you
thawed straight from LN2 temperatures. Another key problem is this:
many structures that are ruptured remain in close physical proximity
to their original locations only so long as the patient is solid -- if
the patient thaws, the structures can float free and all the
positional information would be lost.

> Isn't the cell machinery all "gummed" up by these molecules?

People have thought about locking up cellular machinery with
substances such as gluteraldehyde (sp?), but to my knowledge no
cryonics organization actually does this.

> Also, you have to 
> repair the cells first before warming up otherwise you will cause more
> damage than the initial freezing, and I doubt any biotech machinery
> can work and survive in a super cooled environment.

This is all correct, as I just (perhaps redundantly) noted.


>    What kind of biotechnology can work at very low temperatures,
> remove cryoprotectants, synthesize new cell machinery if possible,
> and repair the circulatory system (which would take coordination)?

None. There are a few organisms that remain active at very high
(boiling) temperatures, but none to my knowledge that are active at
temperatures substantially below -50 degrees C. I may be slightly
wrong on this, but I feel extremely confident that there are no
organisms at all that operate below -100 degrees C, let alone below
the glass transition temperature for cryonics patients, which is
-130C.

Perry

